    passageText:
      'The pressure in a fluid at rest increases with depth. For two points separated by a vertical distance $h$ in a fluid of density $\\rho$, the lower point is at the higher pressure, and the difference between them is $\\rho g h$. Blood is a fluid, and although the circulation is never at rest, this hydrostatic contribution is superimposed on the pressure generated by the heart at every point in the vascular tree. Clinically, blood pressures are reported as gauge pressures, the amount by which the pressure exceeds the surrounding atmospheric pressure, and they are expressed in millimeters of mercury (mmHg), the height of a column of mercury that the pressure can support. One standard atmosphere supports a mercury column 760 mm high; mercury is used for this purpose because it is far denser than water or blood, so the column stays conveniently short.\n\nA sphygmomanometer measures arterial pressure indirectly. An inflatable cuff is wrapped around the upper arm and inflated until it squeezes the brachial artery shut. As the cuff is slowly deflated, the cuff pressure at which blood first spurts through with each heartbeat is recorded as the systolic pressure, and the cuff pressure at which flow becomes continuous and silent is recorded as the diastolic pressure. At each of these moments the pressure inside the cuff equals the pressure inside the artery beneath it, so the cuff gauge reports the arterial pressure at the position of the cuff. By convention the measurement is made with the cuff at the level of the heart.\n\nTo examine how posture affects pressure in different parts of the arterial tree, investigators recorded mean arterial pressure in eight healthy adults using miniature pressure transducers mounted on the tips of catheters threaded into the arterial system. Readings were taken at eight vertical levels, from 100 cm below the heart (at the ankle) to 40 cm above it (at the level of the head), first with each subject lying supine on a horizontal table and then after the subject had stood quietly for five minutes. Each transducer was calibrated against a water manometer before use. The results are shown in Figure 1, in which the vertical position of each site is given relative to the heart, with positive values above it. Mean pressure at heart level happened to be nearly identical in the two postures.\n\nThe investigators compared their findings with two extreme cases. The giraffe carries its brain about 2.0 m above its heart, and its mean arterial pressure at heart level, roughly 250 mmHg, is about two and a half times the human value; the carotid arteries run almost vertically from heart to head. In orbiting astronauts, by contrast, blood has no weight, and the hydrostatic component of pressure vanishes throughout the body. Within hours of reaching orbit, astronauts develop puffy faces and thin legs as fluid redistributes toward the head, and many report nasal congestion like that of a head cold.\n\nVenous pressures are subject to the same hydrostatic gradient, but the veins of the lower limb contain one-way valves, and contraction of the surrounding skeletal muscle during walking drives blood toward the heart one segment at a time, so that venous pressure in the foot during walking is far lower than the full hydrostatic value reached during motionless standing.',
