      'Iron, copper, and zinc are required in milligram quantities by every human cell, yet none of them is allowed to circulate as a free aqueous ion. Each cation is a Lewis acid: it has empty valence orbitals and accepts electron pairs from ligands, which are Lewis bases, to form coordination complexes. In water a bare metal ion is immediately surrounded by water molecules, and the biological chemistry of the metal consists largely of exchanging these water ligands for the nitrogen, oxygen, and sulfur donor atoms of proteins and small molecules. Iron(III) prefers six ligands in an octahedral arrangement, copper(II) is commonly found with four to six, and zinc(II) in enzymes is most often four-coordinate and tetrahedral.\n\nThe three metals differ sharply in their redox behavior. Iron cycles between the +2 and +3 states, and copper between +1 and +2, under conditions found in cells; this is why both serve as electron carriers in the respiratory chain and in oxidases. Zinc(II), with a completely filled 3d subshell, has no second oxidation state that is accessible in biology. It is therefore used where a Lewis acid is needed without any possibility of electron transfer, as in carbonic anhydrase and in the zinc-finger domains of transcription factors.\n\nThe same ability to transfer electrons makes free iron and copper dangerous. In the Fenton reaction, Fe²⁺ + H₂O₂ → Fe³⁺ + OH⁻ + •OH, a trace of reduced iron converts hydrogen peroxide, a routine product of metabolism, into the hydroxyl radical, the most reactive oxygen species encountered in biology; Cu⁺ does the same. For this reason iron in plasma is carried by transferrin, two Fe³⁺ ions per protein molecule, and stored inside ferritin, while copper is carried by ceruloplasmin. In healthy plasma the concentration of free, exchangeable iron is below $10^{-18}$ M.\n\nA ligand that offers two or more donor atoms to the same metal ion is a chelating agent, and the ring that it closes with the metal is a chelate ring. Ethylenediaminetetraacetate, EDTA⁴⁻, is hexadentate: its two amine nitrogens and four carboxylate oxygens occupy all six positions around an octahedral ion. The formation constant $K_f$ for a 1:1 metal–EDTA complex ranges over many orders of magnitude depending on the metal. Representative values of $\\log K_f$ are: Mg²⁺, 8.7; Ca²⁺, 10.7; Zn²⁺, 16.5; Pb²⁺, 18.0; Cu²⁺, 18.8; Fe³⁺, 25.1. In every case the chelate is far more stable than the complex the same metal forms with six separate ligands that each supply one comparable donor atom, even though the individual metal–donor bonds are of similar strength.\n\nChelating agents are the drugs of choice when a metal accumulates to toxic levels. Deferoxamine, a bacterial product bearing three hydroxamic acid groups, binds Fe³⁺ with $\\log K_f$ of about 31 and is given for iron overload. Penicillamine, which carries a thiol, an amine, and a carboxylate, is used for copper overload. For lead poisoning, EDTA is administered not as the disodium salt but as the calcium disodium salt, CaNa₂EDTA, in which the chelator is already occupied by Ca²⁺ when it enters the bloodstream; the disodium salt itself can precipitate a dangerous fall in plasma calcium. Because every one of these drugs binds several essential metals in addition to the one targeted, chelation therapy is monitored with serial measurements of plasma metal concentrations, and mineral supplements may be required during a long course of treatment.',
